# LookML: defining a semantic model (view) over a raw table
view: orders {
  sql_table_name: analytics.orders ;;

  dimension: order_id {
    primary_key: yes
    type: string
    sql: ${TABLE}.order_id ;;
  }

  dimension: order_date {
    type: date
    sql: ${TABLE}.order_date ;;
  }

  measure: total_revenue {
    type: sum
    sql: ${TABLE}.revenue_usd ;;
    # Centralized business logic: revenue excludes refunded orders
    filters: [orders.status: "-refunded"]
  }

  measure: order_count {
    type: count
  }
}

# Explore: defines how views join together (centralizing correct
# join logic so analysts never need to write it themselves)
explore: orders {
  join: customers {
    sql_on: ${orders.customer_id} = ${customers.customer_id} ;;
    relationship: many_to_one
  }
}

Benefit for AI integration:
  An AI natural-language query tool, rather than generating raw
  SQL with potentially incorrect joins or business logic
  (the SQL hallucination risk covered in Genie material elsewhere),
  can be pointed at the LookML semantic layer's already-validated
  dimensions and measures, generating queries against this
  governed layer instead -- inheriting LookML's centralized
  correctness rather than re-deriving join logic and business
  rules from scratch for every AI-generated query
